Replace chained block alignment with an absolute anchor, skip clipped frames

Two fixes for the stack that came out with the Moon cut by a straight edge.

**The regression was mine.** In the previous commit I replaced aligning every
block against the first with a chain (block to previous block, shifts added
up). In principle that keeps each step small and reliable. In practice a single
failed correlation injected a large error that was never corrected, and after a
few of those the object walked off the crop. Measured on the output: the bright
bbox reached x=0, i.e. the Moon was cut by the left edge of the crop. The
clamp I had put on each step allowed a quarter of the crop, which is far larger
than any physical drift between two blocks.

The merge now uses an **absolute anchor**: each block's object centre is
measured by the detector, and the shift is the difference from the first
block's centre. There is no accumulation, so there is nothing to accumulate
wrong. It is also less code and less state. The bbox centre is pixel-accurate,
which is ample: each block is already internally aligned sub-pixel, and the
merge only has to place the blocks.

Adds `SkipReason::ObjectClipped`: a frame whose object touches a sensor edge is
skipped, because part of it was never captured. Aligned onto the reference, its
missing side shows as a straight cut in the stack. Measured on the session, a
few percent of frames are affected.

Tests: 23 pass, including a new one asserting clipping is detected at an edge
and not on a centred object.
